WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.339
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:08.770
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:17.736
Unit thirty-eight: Risk and Uncertainty. In this unit, you will discuss how individuals and organisations deal with risk.

4
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.914
risk assessment.

5
00:00:20.614 --> 00:00:26.189
risk assessment means identifying and evaluating possible dangers.

6
00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:29.777
The school carried out a risk assessment before the trip.

7
00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:33.768
Your turn. Say: risk assessment.

8
00:00:36.468 --> 00:00:37.690
risk assessment.

9
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:39.882
probability.

10
00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:43.626
probability means how likely something is to happen.

11
00:00:43.926 --> 00:00:47.354
The probability of rain today is seventy percent.

12
00:00:48.054 --> 00:00:50.799
Your turn. Say: probability.

13
00:00:53.499 --> 00:00:54.535
probability.

14
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.387
worst-case scenario.

15
00:00:58.087 --> 00:01:02.420
worst-case scenario means the most serious possible outcome.

16
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.473
In the worst-case scenario, the project could be cancelled.

17
00:01:07.173 --> 00:01:10.708
Your turn. Say: worst-case scenario.

18
00:01:13.408 --> 00:01:14.873
worst-case scenario.

19
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:17.477
precautionary principle.

20
00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:25.772
precautionary principle means the idea that action should be taken to prevent harm even without full certainty.

21
00:01:26.072 --> 00:01:29.430
The precautionary principle guided the ban on the chemical.

22
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:33.804
Your turn. Say: precautionary principle.

23
00:01:36.504 --> 00:01:38.155
precautionary principle.

24
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.151
black swan.

25
00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:45.765
black swan means a rare, unpredictable event with huge consequences.

26
00:01:46.065 --> 00:01:49.678
The pandemic was a black swan for the tourism industry.

27
00:01:50.378 --> 00:01:53.367
Your turn. Say: black swan.

28
00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:57.184
black swan.

29
00:01:58.183 --> 00:01:59.359
risk appetite.

30
00:02:00.059 --> 00:02:05.228
risk appetite means how much risk a person or organisation is willing to accept.

31
00:02:05.528 --> 00:02:08.955
Start-ups usually have a high risk appetite.

32
00:02:09.655 --> 00:02:13.271
Your turn. Say: risk appetite.

33
00:02:15.971 --> 00:02:17.297
risk appetite.

34
00:02:18.283 --> 00:02:19.714
contingency plan.

35
00:02:20.414 --> 00:02:24.666
contingency plan means a plan for what to do if something goes wrong.

36
00:02:24.966 --> 00:02:28.289
Every event needs a contingency plan for bad weather.

37
00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:32.837
Your turn. Say: contingency plan.

38
00:02:35.537 --> 00:02:37.026
contingency plan.

39
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:39.691
uncertainty principle.

40
00:02:40.391 --> 00:02:46.141
uncertainty principle means in physics, the limit on how precisely some quantities can be known.

41
00:02:46.441 --> 00:02:52.074
Heisenberg's uncertainty principle is often used as a metaphor for unknowable risks.

42
00:02:52.774 --> 00:02:56.158
Your turn. Say: uncertainty principle.

43
00:02:58.858 --> 00:03:00.358
uncertainty principle.

44
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.593
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.893 --> 00:03:07.820
Ms. Sarah, how do companies plan for things they can't predict?

46
00:03:09.117 --> 00:03:13.334
They carry out a risk assessment and draw up contingency plans.

47
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.857
But what about black swans, like the pandemic?

48
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.078
Those are hard to foresee. Still, firms that had built up reserves pulled through.

49
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:27.464
So it comes down to risk appetite?

50
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:33.097
Partly. Some take on huge risks; others play it safe.

51
00:03:34.383 --> 00:03:35.593
And governments?

52
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:42.633
They often fall back on the precautionary principle: act early, even without certainty.

53
00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:48.389
Grammar focus: Advanced phrasal verbs (extended).

54
00:03:48.689 --> 00:03:51.223
Phrasal verbs are common in business English.

55
00:03:51.523 --> 00:03:55.763
Draw up a plan, pull through a crisis, and fall back on a principle.

56
00:03:56.063 --> 00:03:58.341
Know their formal equivalents for writing.

57
00:03:58.641 --> 00:04:07.943
Many phrasal verbs have formal equivalents: draw up = prepare; pull through = survive; fall back on = rely on.

58
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.183
Now read and listen. Thinking About the Unthinkable.

59
00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:17.629
Every decision involves some degree of risk and uncertainty.

60
00:04:18.917 --> 00:04:25.618
Organisations carry out risk assessments to estimate the probability and impact of possible problems.

61
00:04:26.917 --> 00:04:32.271
They then draw up contingency plans for the most likely and most damaging scenarios.

62
00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:39.089
However, some events, known as black swans, are almost impossible to predict.

63
00:04:40.383 --> 00:04:47.224
The 2008 financial crisis and the COVID-19 pandemic are often given as examples.

64
00:04:48.517 --> 00:04:54.115
Companies with strong reserves and flexible strategies were more likely to pull through.

65
00:04:55.417 --> 00:05:00.806
Governments frequently fall back on the precautionary principle when facing uncertain threats.

66
00:05:02.117 --> 00:05:07.785
Accepting uncertainty, rather than ignoring it, may be the wisest strategy of all.

67
00:05:09.083 --> 00:05:12.038
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:12.338 --> 00:05:17.089
A rare, unpredictable event with huge consequences is a blank.

69
00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:21.715
A, black swan. B, risk assessment. C, probability.

70
00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:27.821
The answer is A: black swan.

71
00:05:29.317 --> 00:05:31.270
The team blank a detailed plan.

72
00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:36.048
A, drew up. B, pulled through. C, fell back.

73
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.991
The answer is A: drew up.

74
00:05:43.483 --> 00:05:45.645
Few businesses blank the crisis.

75
00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:49.610
A, took on. B, pulled through. C, drew up.

76
00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:55.581
The answer is B: pulled through.

77
00:05:57.083 --> 00:06:00.464
A plan for what to do if something goes wrong is a blank.

78
00:06:00.764 --> 00:06:06.368
A, contingency plan. B, risk appetite. C, worst-case scenario.

79
00:06:10.383 --> 00:06:13.242
The answer is A: contingency plan.

80
00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.967
According to the text, which companies were more likely to pull through?

81
00:06:19.267 --> 00:06:25.486
A, those with strong reserves. B, the largest ones. C, the newest ones.

82
00:06:29.483 --> 00:06:32.458
The answer is A: those with strong reserves.

83
00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:39.760
Now it's your turn. Part one: How do you personally deal with risk in important decisions?

84
00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:51.216
Part two: Use two phrasal verbs, describe your approach, and give a real example.

85
00:06:57.516 --> 00:07:12.301
I usually draw up a list of pros and cons and think about the worst-case scenario. When I chose my university, I took on extra risk by moving to Phnom Penh, but I had a contingency plan: I could fall back on my family's support.

86
00:07:12.601 --> 00:07:14.624
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:15.617 --> 00:07:20.754
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced phrasal verbs (extended).

88
00:07:21.054 --> 00:07:25.099
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

89
00:07:25.399 --> 00:07:28.969
See you in Unit thirty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:29.269 --> 00:07:32.037
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

